<body>
    <h1>AI-263: F1-Score Optimization</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p>The F1-score is a single metric that combines precision and reca=
ll to provide a balanced measure of a model's accuracy. It is particularly =
useful when the training set has an uneven class distribution (i.e., one cl=
ass has significantly more samples than the other), as it considers both fa=
lse positives and false negatives.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Precision:</strong>&nbsp;Also known as positive predictive value=
, it measures the proportion of correctly identified positive cases (true p=
ositives) out of all cases predicted as positive (true positives + false po=
sitives). It answers the question: "Of all the faces the model claimed were=
 a match, how many were actually correct?"</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Formula:&nbsp;<code>Precision =3D True Positives / (True Positives + Fal=
se Positives)</code></p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Recall:</strong>&nbsp;Also known as sensitivity or true positive=
 rate, it measures the proportion of correctly identified positive cases (t=
rue positives) out of all actual positive cases (true positives + false neg=
atives). It answers the question: "Of all the actual matches in the dataset=
, how many did the model correctly identify?"</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Formula:&nbsp;<code>Recall =3D True Positives / (True Positives + False =
Negatives)</code></p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>F1-score:</strong>&nbsp;The harmonic mean of precision and recal=
l. It gives a higher weight to lower values, so a high F1-score ensures tha=
t both precision and recall are reasonably high.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Formula:&nbsp;<code>F1-score =3D 2 * (Precision * Recall) / (Precision +=
 Recall)</code></p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>The F1-score ranges from 0 to 1.</p></li>
<li>
<p>An F1-score of 1 indicates perfect precision and recall (the model has n=
o false positives and no false negatives).</p></li>
<li>
<p>An F1-score of 0 indicates that either precision or recall is zero (the =
model is either making a lot of false positive predictions or missing many =
actual positives).</p></li>
</ul>
<p>So why is the F1-score important?</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Balances Precision and Recall:</strong>&nbsp;If we only consider=
 precision, we might choose a model that makes very few positive prediction=
s, resulting in high precision but low recall. Conversely, if you only cons=
ider recall, you might choose a model that predicts everything as positive,=
 resulting in high recall but low precision. The F1-score helps you find a =
balance between these two extremes.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Useful for Imbalanced Datasets:</strong>&nbsp;In many real-world=
 scenarios, the classes are not evenly distributed. For example, in face re=
cognition, there might be far more images of different people than images o=
f the same person. In such cases, accuracy alone can be misleading, and the=
 F1-score provides a more informative metric.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>In the context of face recognition similarity thresholds, we want to cho=
ose a threshold that maximizes the F1-score on your validation set. This me=
ans finding a threshold that gives you a good balance between correctly ide=
ntifying matches (high recall) and avoiding false matches (high precision).=
</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-CoreConcepts">Core Concepts</h1>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-TrainingData">Training Data</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Quantity and Quality:</strong> The performance of a face recogni=
tion model heavily depends on the quantity and quality of the training data=
. More data generally leads to better performance, but it's equally importa=
nt to ensure that the data is clean, diverse, and representative of the fac=
es the system will encounter in real-world use.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Diversity:</strong> The training data should include faces with =
varying poses, lighting conditions, expressions, ages, and demographic char=
acteristics.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Balance:</strong> While perfect balance is not always achievable=
, it's desirable to have a relatively balanced representation of different =
demographic groups in the training data to mitigate bias.</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-ValidationData">Validation Data</h2>
<p>A well-constructed validation set is crucial for evaluating the performa=
nce of a face recognition system and tuning parameters such as similarity t=
hresholds.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Representativeness:</strong> The validation set should be repres=
entative of the real-world scenarios in which the system will be used. This=
 includes matching the distribution of ethnicities, ages, lighting conditio=
ns, image quality, and other relevant factors.</p>
<p><em>Avoid using images that were used in the training set.</em></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Pairwise Comparisons:</strong> The validation process involves c=
reating pairs of face embeddings and labeling them as "match" (same person)=
 or "non-match" (different person).</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Balanced Ratio:</strong> Aim for a roughly 1:1 (50% match, 50% n=
on-match) ratio of match to non-match pairs in the validation set to avoid =
biasing the evaluation towards either positive or negative cases.</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-EvaluationMetrics:Precision,Recall,an=
dF1-Score">Evaluation Metrics: Precision, Recall, and F1-Score</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Precision:</strong> Measures the proportion of correctly identif=
ied positive cases (true positives) out of all cases predicted as positive =
(true positives + false positives). High precision means fewer false matche=
s.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Formula: Precision =3D True Positives / (True Positives + False Positive=
s)</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Recall:</strong> Measures the proportion of correctly identified=
 positive cases (true positives) out of all actual positive cases (true pos=
itives + false negatives). High recall means fewer missed matches.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Formula: Recall =3D True Positives / (True Positives + False Negatives)<=
/p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>F1-Score:</strong> The harmonic mean of precision and recall, pr=
oviding a balanced measure of accuracy. It is particularly useful when deal=
ing with imbalanced datasets.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Formula: F1-score =3D 2 * (Precision * Recall) / (Precision + Recall)</p=
></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<h1 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-CreatingValidationSets:PracticalConsi=
derations">Creating Validation Sets: Practical Considerations</h1>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-GeneralGuidelines">General Guidelines=
</h2>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Separate Validation Set:</strong> Always use a separate validati=
on set that is distinct from the training data.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Sufficient Size:</strong> The validation set should be large eno=
ugh to provide a statistically meaningful assessment of the model's perform=
ance. The size is also dictated by number of classes you need to test. For =
example, to reliably test the F1 score of a model's effectiveness with X im=
ages per class in the test dataset to evaluate, then at least X*(number of =
classes) samples are needed in the validation dataset.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Real-World Scenarios:</strong> The data in the validation set sh=
ould reflect the real-world conditions the system will encounter. If the sy=
stem will primarily be used on images from security cameras, the validation=
 data should include such images. If you are recognizing baseball players, =
you need to find images of baseball players.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Diversity:</strong> The data should include diversity of faces. =
Ensure you have enough different faces to be representative of the real dep=
loyment environment.</p></li>
</ol>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-HandlingDemographicDiversity">Handlin=
g Demographic Diversity</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Representative Sampling:</strong> The demographic composition of=
 the validation set should reflect the intended use case of the face recogn=
ition system. If the system will be used on a diverse population, the valid=
ation set should also be diverse, with a representative sample of different=
 races/ethnicities, genders, ages, etc.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Bias Detection:</strong> A balanced dataset is not sufficient to=
 measure bias. Create subsets for each race/ethnicity to see the performanc=
e of the system across all demographics.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Inclusion of Similar Faces</strong>: It's vital to include faces=
 of the same ethnicity to determine whether they can properly differentiate=
 between two faces, in addition to measuring bias.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-TheN-PlayerProblemandthe&quot;ShoheiO=
htani&quot;Example">The <em>N</em>-Player Problem and the "Shohei Ohtani" E=
xample</h3>
<p>To use a practical example, all of this comes from working on the LA Dod=
gers training set. To effectively validate a face recognition model, consid=
er a scenario where a training set includes multiple players from a sports =
team. To validate the data for Shohei Ohtani, the validation dataset should=
:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Include other players with Japanese origin.</strong></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Include players that are not of Japanese origin</strong></p></li=
>
</ol>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-GeneratingValidationPairs">Generating=
 Validation Pairs</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Pairwise Comparisons:</strong> Create pairs of images from the v=
alidation set.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Match and Non-Match Labeling:</strong> Label each pair as "match=
" (same person) or "non-match" (different person).</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Equal Proportions:</strong> If possible, ensure there are an equ=
al amount of positive and negative labels.</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-OptimizingtheF1-Score">Optimizing the=
 F1-Score</h2>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-SimilarityThresholds">Similarity Thre=
sholds</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Model-Specific Tuning:</strong> Different face recognition model=
s produce embeddings with different characteristics. Therefore, the optimal=
 similarity threshold for determining a match will vary from model to model=
.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Threshold Optimization:</strong> Iterate through a range of thre=
shold values and calculate precision, recall, and F1-score on the validatio=
n set for each threshold. Choose the threshold that maximizes the F1-score =
or provides the desired balance between precision and recall.</p></li>
<li>
<p><em>Remember to analyze this data for all demographic groups to detect b=
ias.</em></p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-Step-by-StepProcessforF1-ScoreOptimiz=
ation">Step-by-Step Process for F1-Score Optimization</h2>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Prepare Validation Data:</strong> See sections above.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Extract Embeddings:</strong> Use the face recognition model to e=
xtract face embeddings for all images in the validation set.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Calculate Similarity Scores:</strong> Calculate the similarity s=
core for each pair of face embeddings in the validation set using the appro=
priate metric (Euclidean distance or cosine similarity).</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Iterate Through Thresholds:</strong> For each model, iterate thr=
ough a range of potential threshold values.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Calculate TP, FP, TN, FN:</strong> For each threshold value, det=
ermine the true positives (TP), false positives (FP), true negatives (TN), =
and false negatives (FN) based on the ground truth labels.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Calculate Precision, Recall, and F1-score:</strong> For each thr=
eshold, calculate precision, recall, and F1-score.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Find Optimal Threshold:</strong> Choose the threshold that maxim=
izes the F1-score or provides the desired balance between precision and rec=
all.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Analyze Results for Different Demographic Subsets</strong>: This=
 can help you understand if the performance of the model is consistent acro=
ss all races/ethnicities.</p></li>
</ol>
<h2 id=3D"AI-263:F1-ScoreOptimization-CodeImplementation(Conceptual)">Code =
Implementation (Conceptual)</h2>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">import nu=
mpy as np
from sklearn.metrics import f1_score, precision_score, recall_score

def calculate_f1_score(embeddings, labels, metric=3D"euclidean", thresholds=
 =3D np.arange(0.0, 1.0, 0.01)):

    # Assuming you have the following:
    # - 'embeddings': A dictionary where keys are image filenames and value=
s are the embedding vectors
    # - 'labels': A dictionary where keys are image filenames and values ar=
e the person's ID (ground truth)
    # - 'metric': "euclidean" or "cosine"

    pairs =3D [] # list of pairs of embeddings
    pair_labels =3D [] # 1 if images are of same class, 0 if not

    # Creating positive pairs:
    num_positive_pairs_to_create =3D 5000
    positive_pairs_created =3D 0
    i=3D0
    while positive_pairs_created &lt; num_positive_pairs_to_create and i &l=
t; len(embeddings):
        j =3D 0
        while positive_pairs_created &lt; num_positive_pairs_to_create and =
j &lt; len(embeddings):
            if labels[i] =3D=3D labels[j]:
                pairs.append((embeddings[i], embeddings[j]))
                pair_labels.append(1)
                positive_pairs_created +=3D1
            j+=3D1
        i +=3D 1

    # Creating negative pairs:
    num_negative_pairs_to_create =3D 5000
    negative_pairs_created =3D 0
    i=3D0
    while negative_pairs_created &lt; num_negative_pairs_to_create and i &l=
t; len(embeddings):
        j =3D 0
        while negative_pairs_created &lt; num_negative_pairs_to_create and =
j &lt; len(embeddings):
            if labels[i] !=3D labels[j]:
                pairs.append((embeddings[i], embeddings[j]))
                pair_labels.append(0)
                negative_pairs_created +=3D1
            j+=3D1
        i +=3D 1

    # Define a function to calculate the distance/similarity based on the m=
etric
    def calculate_similarity(emb1, emb2, metric):
        if metric =3D=3D "euclidean":
            return np.linalg.norm(emb1 - emb2)
        elif metric =3D=3D "cosine":
            dot_product =3D np.dot(emb1, emb2)
            norm_emb1 =3D np.linalg.norm(emb1)
            norm_emb2 =3D np.linalg.norm(emb2)
            return dot_product / (norm_emb1 * norm_emb2)
        else:
            raise ValueError("Unsupported metric")

    # Calculate scores
    scores =3D [calculate_similarity(pair[0], pair[1], metric) for pair in =
pairs]

    # Find optimal threshold by maximizing F1-score
    best_threshold =3D None
    best_f1_score =3D -1

    for threshold in thresholds:
        # Apply threshold and get predictions
        if metric =3D=3D "euclidean":
            predictions =3D [1 if score &lt;=3D threshold else 0] * len(sco=
res)
        elif metric =3D=3D "cosine":
            predictions =3D [1 if score &gt;=3D threshold else 0] * len(sco=
res)

        # Calculate F1-score, precision, and recall
        f1 =3D f1_score(pair_labels, predictions)
        precision =3D precision_score(pair_labels, predictions)
        recall =3D recall_score(pair_labels, predictions)

        if f1 &gt; best_f1_score:
            best_f1_score =3D f1
            best_threshold =3D threshold

    return best_threshold, best_f1_score

# Example usage:
# best_threshold, best_f1_score =3D calculate_f1_score(embeddings, labels, =
metric=3D"euclidean")
# print(f"Best Threshold: {best_threshold}, Best F1-score: {best_f1_score}"=
)
